    {
      "id": "a3Lq-vD9_eXv7IPympUoF",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -16798.910503554464,
      "y": 137.98140478171695,
      "width": 1186.1390380859375,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 40027956,
      "version": 696,
      "versionNonce": 1209475636,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783830767831,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "APIs\n\n// get a list of problems\nGET /problems?page=1&limit=100 -> Partial<Problem>[]\n\nNote : Partial here is taken from TypeScript it indicates only a subset of the \nproblem entity is returned because we don't want to see everyting\nabout a problem in a list, maybe id, title, tag, category etc.\n\n\n// get a problem\nGET /problems/:id?language={language} -> Problem\n\nthis returns full problem statement and code stub, if language not provided then default to any\n\n\n// submit a problem\nPOST /problems/:id/submit -> Submission\n{\n  code: string,\n  language: string\n}\n\n\n// view live leaderboard\nGET /leaderboard/:competitionId?page=1&limit=100 -> Leaderboard\n\n\nNote : user data always through JWT/session tokens and don't pass TS from client, they should be generated by server.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "APIs\n\n// get a list of problems\nGET /problems?page=1&limit=100 -> Partial<Problem>[]\n\nNote : Partial here is taken from TypeScript it indicates only a subset of the \nproblem entity is returned because we don't want to see everyting\nabout a problem in a list, maybe id, title, tag, category etc.\n\n\n// get a problem\nGET /problems/:id?language={language} -> Problem\n\nthis returns full problem statement and code stub, if language not provided then default to any\n\n\n// submit a problem\nPOST /problems/:id/submit -> Submission\n{\n  code: string,\n  language: string\n}\n\n\n// view live leaderboard\nGET /leaderboard/:competitionId?page=1&limit=100 -> Leaderboard\n\n\nNote : user data always through JWT/session tokens and don't pass TS from client, they should be generated by server.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "gpolvMHpxvR6ZnlsRRucm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -17482.36451058927,
      "y": 922.2869486198473,
      "width": 1703.259033203125,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 495246220,
      "version": 661,
      "versionNonce": 62591372,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783828943342,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Notes\n- Generally prefer micro-services but for this a simple monolith is sufficient as user base is small scale is not that high.\n- SQL or NoSQL both works here, but lets user NoSQL DynamoDB here as complex queries are not needed and we can nest testcases as a subdocument in the problem entity.\n- Test cases can generally could be huge you can either use a S3 or a separate DB to store them using problemID\n- eg\n        {\n          id: string,\n          title: string,\n          question: string,\n          level: string,\n          tags: string[],\n          codeStubs: {\n            python: string,\n            javascript: string,\n            typescript: string,\n            ...\n          },\n          testCases: {\n            type: string,\n            input: JSON,\n            output: JSON\n          }[]\n        }\n- We'll use Monaco Editor to allow user to code their solution in browser.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Notes\n- Generally prefer micro-services but for this a simple monolith is sufficient as user base is small scale is not that high.\n- SQL or NoSQL both works here, but lets user NoSQL DynamoDB here as complex queries are not needed and we can nest testcases as a subdocument in the problem entity.\n- Test cases can generally could be huge you can either use a S3 or a separate DB to store them using problemID\n- eg\n        {\n          id: string,\n          title: string,\n          question: string,\n          level: string,\n          tags: string[],\n          codeStubs: {\n            python: string,\n            javascript: string,\n            typescript: string,\n            ...\n          },\n          testCases: {\n            type: string,\n            input: JSON,\n            output: JSON\n          }[]\n        }\n- We'll use Monaco Editor to allow user to code their solution in browser.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "tnqTY4CXAbHaqpJv3IjxO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -17577.546053157632,
      "y": 1582.0680959684464,
      "width": 2602.6181640625,
      "height": 1050,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 787049740,
      "version": 3590,
      "versionNonce": 120518708,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783830305158,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Users should be able to submit their solution and get instant feedback\n- users code needs to be run on a machine against test cases to produce feedback\n\n1. Bad Solution : Run Code in API Server\n- this means saving the code file in the primary server local filesystem, running it and capturing the output\n- Terrible Idea - Never run client code on your API server directly, Huge security concern\n\nProblems\n    - Malicious code submitted, it could take down your entire server. eg. deletes all your data or sends all your data to a third party.\n    - Could also use your server to mine cryptocurrency, launch DDoS attack etc. using your resources since you gave them access to it.\n    - Another issue is running large number of such code in parallel is CPU intensive could crash your system, say code is in infinite loop or has a memory leak\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Run Code in VM\n- spawn a VM on the API server and run the code, its isolated, doesn't affect the primary server if something goes wrong, easily resets.\n\nProblems\n    - VMs are resource intensive and slow to start-up\n    - Need to quickly spin-up new VM when load increases - Needs time to boot up!\n    - Lifecycle management of VM are tough and you don't want them to be running when needed.\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Run Code in a Container(Docker)\n- Much light weight than a VM, faster to startup\n- VMs runs on a physical hardware through a hypervisor(VMware or Hyper-V), each VM is a full copy of an OS, apps, necessary binaries, libs making them large in size and slow to start. Are fully isolated, running an entire OS stack, strong isolation and security.\n- Containers share the host system's Kernel and isolate app processes from each other. They include the app and its dependencies(libs, binaries) not full OS making them light weight and faster, offers less isolation but more efficient in resource usage and scalability.\n- Create a container for each runtime we support(Python, JS, Java) that has all the necessary dependencies installed and just run the client code when ever submitted, same container gets reused, and scale if sumbmissions increase\n\nProblems : host OS kernel shared, need to properly secure container so that they don't break out of the container to access the host system\nEnforce resource limits\n\n\n4. Great Solution : Run Code in a Serverless Function(AWS Lambda)\n- Serverless functions are small, stateless, event-driven functions that run in response to triggers (e.g. an HTTP request).\n- They are managed by a cloud provider and automatically scale up or down based on demand, making them a great option for running code that is CPU intensive or unpredictable in terms of load.\n- Create serverless function for each runtime we support that installs necessary dependencies and run code in a sandbox env, when user submits their code we trigger appropriate serverless func to run code and return result.\n\nProblem\n    - Cold start time : Preparing the function i.e. getting the env ready, installing dependencies etc.\n    - Introduces latency for the first request\n    - They have resource limits and impacts performance of long running or intensive code tasks\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Users should be able to submit their solution and get instant feedback\n- users code needs to be run on a machine against test cases to produce feedback\n\n1. Bad Solution : Run Code in API Server\n- this means saving the code file in the primary server local filesystem, running it and capturing the output\n- Terrible Idea - Never run client code on your API server directly, Huge security concern\n\nProblems\n    - Malicious code submitted, it could take down your entire server. eg. deletes all your data or sends all your data to a third party.\n    - Could also use your server to mine cryptocurrency, launch DDoS attack etc. using your resources since you gave them access to it.\n    - Another issue is running large number of such code in parallel is CPU intensive could crash your system, say code is in infinite loop or has a memory leak\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Run Code in VM\n- spawn a VM on the API server and run the code, its isolated, doesn't affect the primary server if something goes wrong, easily resets.\n\nProblems\n    - VMs are resource intensive and slow to start-up\n    - Need to quickly spin-up new VM when load increases - Needs time to boot up!\n    - Lifecycle management of VM are tough and you don't want them to be running when needed.\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Run Code in a Container(Docker)\n- Much light weight than a VM, faster to startup\n- VMs runs on a physical hardware through a hypervisor(VMware or Hyper-V), each VM is a full copy of an OS, apps, necessary binaries, libs making them large in size and slow to start. Are fully isolated, running an entire OS stack, strong isolation and security.\n- Containers share the host system's Kernel and isolate app processes from each other. They include the app and its dependencies(libs, binaries) not full OS making them light weight and faster, offers less isolation but more efficient in resource usage and scalability.\n- Create a container for each runtime we support(Python, JS, Java) that has all the necessary dependencies installed and just run the client code when ever submitted, same container gets reused, and scale if sumbmissions increase\n\nProblems : host OS kernel shared, need to properly secure container so that they don't break out of the container to access the host system\nEnforce resource limits\n\n\n4. Great Solution : Run Code in a Serverless Function(AWS Lambda)\n- Serverless functions are small, stateless, event-driven functions that run in response to triggers (e.g. an HTTP request).\n- They are managed by a cloud provider and automatically scale up or down based on demand, making them a great option for running code that is CPU intensive or unpredictable in terms of load.\n- Create serverless function for each runtime we support that installs necessary dependencies and run code in a sandbox env, when user submits their code we trigger appropriate serverless func to run code and return result.\n\nProblem\n    - Cold start time : Preparing the function i.e. getting the env ready, installing dependencies etc.\n    - Introduces latency for the first request\n    - They have resource limits and impacts performance of long running or intensive code tasks\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
