      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aD",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1055791244,
      "version": 1153,
      "versionNonce": 1868464396,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783832651445,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to ensure isolation and security when running user code?\n- Container/Lambda already ensured that\n- Read only filesystem : mount code directory as read-only and write output to a temp directory that is deleted short after\n- CPU and Memory bounds : set limit on the container, this prevents user for consuming excessive resources and the container/process gets killed if exceeded.\n- Explicit Timeouts : This prevents users from running infinite timeouts, 5s is good timeout, meeting our requirement of returning result in 5s\n- Limit Network Access : disable network access in a container, prevents users from making external calls. In AWS we use Virtual Private Cloud(VPC) security groups and NACLs to restrict all outbound and inbound allowing only few predefined and essential connections.\n- No System Calls : use seccomp to restrict container from making system calls.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to ensure isolation and security when running user code?\n- Container/Lambda already ensured that\n- Read only filesystem : mount code directory as read-only and write output to a temp directory that is deleted short after\n- CPU and Memory bounds : set limit on the container, this prevents user for consuming excessive resources and the container/process gets killed if exceeded.\n- Explicit Timeouts : This prevents users from running infinite timeouts, 5s is good timeout, meeting our requirement of returning result in 5s\n- Limit Network Access : disable network access in a container, prevents users from making external calls. In AWS we use Virtual Private Cloud(VPC) security groups and NACLs to restrict all outbound and inbound allowing only few predefined and essential connections.\n- No System Calls : use seccomp to restrict container from making system calls.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "p77XiAWGJVeMamfBGko4W",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -14817.830060140424,
      "y": 332.5324946320339,
      "width": 1329.1192626953125,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aE",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 398003340,
      "version": 1655,
      "versionNonce": 1347967796,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783833585990,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to make leaderboard fetch more efficient?\n\n1. Bad Solution : Polling with DB Queries\n- The one we have currently, running expensive query \n- High DB load, increased latency when users increase, doesn't scale well\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Caching with Periodic Updates\n- Use Redis to store previous approach's query output, update it periodically sya 30s by querying DB\n- When client requests, return output from Redis\n\nProblem : Updates aren't truly real-time, polling from DB still involved.\nPotential race conditions if cache update frequency is too low(query executes in 10s, cache TTL is 8s)\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Redis Sorted Set with Periodic Polling\n- Use Redis's sorted set to maintain a real-time leaderboard and storing submission result in main DB\n- When a submission is processed, both DB and redis sorted set are updated.\n- Sorted set is DS in Redis allowing us to sort entries based on a score\n- Sorted set key -> competition:leaderboard:{competitionId}, score could be calculated based on nos of prob solved and time taken etc.\n- update user submission in redis -> ZADD competition:leaderboard:{competitionId} {score} {userId}\n- retrieve the top N users -> ZRANGE competition:leaderboard:{competitionId} 0 N-1 REV WITHSCORES\n\n- No need to use websockets as fetching became real fast, periodic polling give near-realtime experience.\n- We could poll more frequently(2s) towards the end of the comp where as every 10s other times.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to make leaderboard fetch more efficient?\n\n1. Bad Solution : Polling with DB Queries\n- The one we have currently, running expensive query \n- High DB load, increased latency when users increase, doesn't scale well\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Caching with Periodic Updates\n- Use Redis to store previous approach's query output, update it periodically sya 30s by querying DB\n- When client requests, return output from Redis\n\nProblem : Updates aren't truly real-time, polling from DB still involved.\nPotential race conditions if cache update frequency is too low(query executes in 10s, cache TTL is 8s)\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Redis Sorted Set with Periodic Polling\n- Use Redis's sorted set to maintain a real-time leaderboard and storing submission result in main DB\n- When a submission is processed, both DB and redis sorted set are updated.\n- Sorted set is DS in Redis allowing us to sort entries based on a score\n- Sorted set key -> competition:leaderboard:{competitionId}, score could be calculated based on nos of prob solved and time taken etc.\n- update user submission in redis -> ZADD competition:leaderboard:{competitionId} {score} {userId}\n- retrieve the top N users -> ZRANGE competition:leaderboard:{competitionId} 0 N-1 REV WITHSCORES\n\n- No need to use websockets as fetching became real fast, periodic polling give near-realtime experience.\n- We could poll more frequently(2s) towards the end of the comp where as every 10s other times.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "kRSxbTiJirfXtFIgWXXbW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -14808.665472478839,
      "y": 1115.5989263975052,
      "width": 1688.27880859375,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aF",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1539323828,
      "version": 2236,
      "versionNonce": 1075444404,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783835064134,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to scale to 100k users?\n- We often can get sudden spikes in submissions towards the end of a competition, overwhelming the container\n\n1. Bad Solution : Vertical Scaling\n- 10k submission(peak), 100 test cases, 100ms to run all test cases -> 10s/submission\n- 10k submissions 10s 1M test cases = 100k seconds ~27Hours we would need 1667CPU cores to handle this load within 1min\n- even AWSs largest instance have few hundred vCPUs\n- Very expensive sitting idle during peak hours, unnecessary billing\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Dynamic Horizontal Scaling\n- Spin up multiple container for each language, if using Docker - Kubernetes is good option\n- AWS use ECS to manage containers and ECS Auto Scaling to adjust containers based on demand\n\nProblem : Risk of over provisioning, but manageable with modern cloud providers.\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Horizontal Scaling with Queue\n- Same approach as above but add a queue b/w API server and containers, allowing us to buffer submissions during peak times ensuring we don't overwhelm the containers\n- SQS or Kafka\n- Worker nodes pull data from queue and sends them to containers for execution this ensures we don't loose any submission during peak hours.\n- Note : this has made our system async, means we can no longer in the same API call return submission result to user, instead they need to poll periodically for the results.\n- A new endpoint GET /check/:id, polled every second by client to check on submission result, if still in progress returns \"progressing\" on UI\n- This is exactly what leetcode does\n- WebSockets could be added but it adds unnecessary complexities.\n- This also helps us to enable retries in event of a container failure, nice to have feature, we simply requeue the submission\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to scale to 100k users?\n- We often can get sudden spikes in submissions towards the end of a competition, overwhelming the container\n\n1. Bad Solution : Vertical Scaling\n- 10k submission(peak), 100 test cases, 100ms to run all test cases -> 10s/submission\n- 10k submissions 10s 1M test cases = 100k seconds ~27Hours we would need 1667CPU cores to handle this load within 1min\n- even AWSs largest instance have few hundred vCPUs\n- Very expensive sitting idle during peak hours, unnecessary billing\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Dynamic Horizontal Scaling\n- Spin up multiple container for each language, if using Docker - Kubernetes is good option\n- AWS use ECS to manage containers and ECS Auto Scaling to adjust containers based on demand\n\nProblem : Risk of over provisioning, but manageable with modern cloud providers.\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Horizontal Scaling with Queue\n- Same approach as above but add a queue b/w API server and containers, allowing us to buffer submissions during peak times ensuring we don't overwhelm the containers\n- SQS or Kafka\n- Worker nodes pull data from queue and sends them to containers for execution this ensures we don't loose any submission during peak hours.\n- Note : this has made our system async, means we can no longer in the same API call return submission result to user, instead they need to poll periodically for the results.\n- A new endpoint GET /check/:id, polled every second by client to check on submission result, if still in progress returns \"progressing\" on UI\n- This is exactly what leetcode does\n- WebSockets could be added but it adds unnecessary complexities.\n- This also helps us to enable retries in event of a container failure, nice to have feature, we simply requeue the submission\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Vzujc6P3W_y4gYSnHr4XL",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -14806.196354216792,
      "y": 1874.7684897632791,
      "width": 1920.6988525390625,
      "height": 75,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1897662260,
      "version": 451,
      "versionNonce": 1943628340,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783835440534,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How system handles running test cases?\n- We maintain a single test file per problem and standardise serialization and deserialization of it such that it works for every language.\n- Have a standard way to serialise the input/output of each test case, for each language deserialise these inputs, pass it to user's code, compare the output to the deserialised expected output.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How system handles running test cases?\n- We maintain a single test file per problem and standardise serialization and deserialization of it such that it works for every language.\n- Have a standard way to serialise the input/output of each test case, for each language deserialise these inputs, pass it to user's code, compare the output to the deserialised expected output.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
